ype/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0fdf0bf-0fdc-570c-aed5-7c3e6ec100e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347327} :answer-field/id #uuid "329b56ad-7508-5224-a7c6-7d7ef0019f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec5db497-36ab-565c-9851-a44645728973" :question/math-academy-id "q-78390" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{t}{2},\\,t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\cdot u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t$,  we have that $t=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6e8a62-6c5e-5c07-8b38-9629f6e61c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\frac{6}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346290} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc9ddc0-286e-5730-8348-630a2c6bfe50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346297} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bcc86-1dfb-5e38-8fa0-9abbc2ce5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346701} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5c653c-10ca-5639-86e6-15c284b3313c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{14},\\,\\frac{3}{14},\\,\\frac{2}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347148} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ec4efb-ec1e-5eee-8d21-72d1fcfd2de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{7},\\,\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bcc86-1dfb-5e38-8fa0-9abbc2ce5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346701} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f562-fc1a-5512-a817-ac49a9aa2b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80d24151-001d-558c-b4cf-13fe29c86835" :question/math-academy-id "q-78403" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,3{e}^{t},\\,6{e}^{t}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7({e}^{t}-1),$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=7({e}^{t}-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{7} & ={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ {e}^{t} & =\\frac{s}{7}+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})) \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,3{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,6{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,6\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2s+14}{7},\\,\\frac{3s+21}{7},\\,\\frac{6s+42}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s+14}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s+21}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+42}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} {:answer/id #uuid "90720291-67d0-5e1a-b546-8a1aea34b932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393852} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcf0b34-e534-51fc-97fb-64d7d6fafa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393853} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc2226-9b81-5440-8648-0026a95bbbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{18},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393854} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdf1ca-35ec-595a-a27c-e608c973c72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{81},\\,\\frac{4}{81},\\,\\frac{8}{81}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fefcfb2-453a-5854-be55-e93605da6418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56897adb-706a-5c81-8930-6f2aa15c0be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78404" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=9{t}^{2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=9{t}^{2}$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{s}{9} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,4{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,8{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{s}{9},\\,\\frac{4s}{9},\\,\\frac{8s}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{8s}{9})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
